Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns and how we will use / filter them  
(event‑level unless otherwise noted)

• `event_date`: filter to the days we need ( '20201101' – '20201130' ) and group‑by day.  
• `event_name`: identify the two GA4 events of interest – ‘page_view’ and ‘purchase’.  
• `user_pseudo_id`: pseudonymous user id – used to ① pick the set of users who purchased and ② compute page‑view averages per user.  
• `_TABLE_SUFFIX` (BigQuery pseudo‑column when using a wildcard on the events_* tables): lets us scan only the November 2020 daily tables (`WHERE _TABLE_SUFFIX BETWEEN '20201101' AND '20201130'`).  

Typical conditions that will appear in the queries  
• `event_name = 'purchase'` – marks a purchase event.  
• `event_name = 'page_view'` – marks a page view.  
• `_TABLE_SUFFIX BETWEEN '20201101' AND '20201130'` – restricts the scan to November 2020.  
• `user_pseudo_id IN ( /* list or sub‑query of purchasers */ )` – keeps only users who purchased in Nov‑2020.

Below are ten exploration queries (simple → complex → final answer).  
All of them query only the tables in bigquery‑public‑data.ga4_obfuscated_sample_ecommerce and are limited to ≤ 20 rows so you can run them safely.

